{"k":"the teacher's page","text":"For the teacher\nPart 1: the flight lasts 0.2–0.45 s. Why can a hand stopwatch not measure it well, and what do you do instead?\nStarting and stopping a stopwatch by hand scatters by about 0.1 s, a third or more of the whole flight. Measure the range with the tape instead (its error is 0.5 mm); the time follows from the height, T = √(2h/g), or from the range, T = L / v₀.\nPart 1: why is the range plotted against √h and not against h?\nThe fall from the height h lasts T = √(2h/g), and meanwhile the ball moves sideways at the constant speed v₀: L = v₀·T = v₀·√(2/g)·√h. So L is proportional to √h: the points of L(√h) lie on a straight
